What it is: A controlled engineering workflow execution layer.

This is arguably one of the more strategically important packages in the group.

Its purpose is to turn a successful engineering task into a repeatable, versioned procedure that can be executed by AgentSam across different applications and hosts.

Imagine:
1. AgentSam receives a request to inspect a CAD project.
2. It selects an approved PrimeTech procedure.
3. The procedure checks the required capabilities.
4. It executes its defined steps.
5. It validates the outputs.
6. It records an evidence receipt containing hashes and execution information.
The same procedure should work through Local Studio, a desktop host, a Cloudflare host or an SDK integration without duplicating its implementation.
What the engine actually contains
Its main JavaScript implementation is:

packages/primetech-procedures/src/index.js

It exposes four important APIs:
API	Purpose
validateProcedureManifest()	Validates procedure definitions
createProcedureCatalog()	Discovers and manages available procedures
createPrimeTechProcedureHandler()	Executes authorized procedures
summarizeProcedureRuns()	Summarizes execution receipts

The procedure engine supports five effect classifications:
- read
- local_write
- project_write
- export
- external_action
A procedure also defines its input schema, output schema, required capabilities, execution environment, verification method and approval requirements.

This is important because the engine doesn't simply execute arbitrary commands supplied by an agent.

It uses existing SAM authorization and operation routing.
Two actual procedures
A. repo.asset.inventory@0.1.0
Read-only
Inspects an engineering project for native assets.

Supported asset types include:
- FreeCAD FCStd
- IFC
- Blender
- STEP
- DXF
- SVG
- GLB
Produces:
- Asset inventory.
- File sizes.
- SHA-256 hashes.
- File-type counts.
- Explicit truncation and error information.
- Deterministic JSON output.
It does not modify project files or require operator review.

B. cad.motor-bearing-end-plate@0.1.0
Requires operator review
This is a much more substantial manufacturing procedure.

It generates a parametric motor/bearing end plate using FreeCAD and OpenCASCADE.

Its output includes:
- Native FCStd model.
- STEP geometry.
- STL geometry.
- Verification and feasibility JSON.
- Dimensioned SVG/PDF drawing.
- Simulation-only CAM plan.
- Illustrative toolpath preview.
- Seven-page PDF review deck.
- Source snapshot.
- Manifest and ZIP delivery bundle.
An interesting engineering decision already documented:

The original 120 mm outer diameter could not accommodate the specified separate pocket while clearing the 52 mm counterbore and remaining inside the circular boundary.

Candidate B uses a 150 mm diameter and a different pocket placement.

That is explicitly recorded as a proposal, not an approved design revision.

The CAM output is not approved for physical machining. No physical hardware is actuated.
What PrimeTech Procedures is intended to cover
Its README identifies six production disciplines:
1. Architecture and BIM.
2. Mechanical and industrial engineering.
3. Spatial worlds.
4. Motion and media.
5. Brands and merchandise.
6. Software and CMS.
These are intended workflow categories, not six fully implemented procedure libraries.

Currently, the package contains two procedure definitions.
Why I would keep it private for now
This is infrastructure, not an end-user application.

There is also a metadata inconsistency:
Manifest	Declared version
package.json	2.6.14
agentsam.package.json	0.1.0

The AgentSam manifest classifies it as experimental-private-workspace.

I would retain the internal package while expanding the verified procedure library. Its long-term value is considerable: it could become the common execution layer for PrimeTech's engineering disciplines.

