What it is: The technical agreement between your CMS and its independently installable website themes.

This is not an actual theme.

It defines how a theme is allowed to represent, render and customize customer-owned content.

This is particularly important for your Theme Store.
The fundamental distinction
Customer owns the data

Products, collections, media, navigation, customers and publishing state.


Theme owns the presentation

Layouts, visual renderers, presets, tokens, motion, section capabilities and default compositions.


Switching themes must not duplicate or destroy customer store data.

This is what allows a customer to switch between Cypress, Ember, Forge, Revise, Summit and the other themes without rebuilding their product catalog.
Its six architectural building blocks
Building block	Responsibility
Component	Reusable visual primitive
Section definition	Describes an editable composition
Section instance	Customer-owned placement of a section
Template definition	Defines a page composition
Layout definition	Defines the outer site frame
Theme package	Bundles visual assets and rendering capabilities

For example:

A Product Hero is a section definition.

A particular customer's Product Hero on a particular page is a section instance.

Cypress and Revise can render that same instance differently.

The instance's stable ID and customer data remain intact.
